 @VincentAmadeus  I have a 7800XT and a 7800X3D and play Stalker 2 at 1440p with Epic settings and native AA at 80-90FPS. I play every single game at max settings at 1440 with great fps, usually up to 100-120fps and there is always headroom to spare with FSR3 if you need to. 

I have not regretted buying a 7800XT even once. It's great cards and probably best value for money at this point. The 7900GRE is also a good buy for slightly more if you can get it, but there is the slight issue of the cards not having the best looking premium coolers and designs to choose from. With a 7800XT Sapphire or Powercolor you get a premium looking card that perform extremely well.. I have a Sapphire Pure and it looks so amazing in my white and black build. You can also get some real bargains for XFX ones. 

But if you want to have a great looking card that plays everything at full settings at 1440p, look no further than a Sapphire 7800XT Pure or Nitro

 @SafetNeziri666  Depends on your budget.  I finally upgraded to XFX Merc 310 black 7900xtx for 899 USD in 2023 October, though the card I bought has not dipped below 969 USD on any sales/PC Partpicker chart that I can see.   Its a great card and I have been happy.

IMO, none of the Nvidia 4000 series cards justify their price to performance this generation. 

I'd lean towards the 7800, but I did not do my research focusing on that card so definitely YMMV.   I will happily jump between the two brands depending on price to performance for the generation I am wanting an upgrade during.

 @navvvix  Except there's currently no game where the performance difference IN FAVOUR OF having more quality matters. Both are equally unplayable for real time usage.

Where the 8gb with the same settings struggle at single digit performance the 16gb version struggles at 2x the performance of what was previously a single digit framerate...

They're completely similar in quality in real time workloads where the performance is where you want it to be & under those circumstances there's literally no need for more than 8gb of VRAM.

 @Bloodline2009  What I'm saying is that the 6700XT was a great card, is still a great card best in it's class, BUT because it's such a strong mid range performer, it kinda means you have to keep hold of it for longer OR make the jump to a high end card as an upgrade.

Same thing happened with the 7870Ghz, R9 280X, RX5600XT, etc.

We are a long way off the 8700XT.

